      <section id="unknowns" data-layer="fact">
        <SectionKicker text="What remains private" />
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>As of September 20, no executed artist, promoter or venue contract was public.</p>

        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>Communications among Kraft, Messina, Sheeran's representatives and each venue were not public. It was not known whether Kraft coordinated broader venue pressure or who made the final decisions at Lincoln Financial Field, Mercedes-Benz Stadium, Lucas Oil Stadium, Bank of America Stadium and AT&T Stadium.</p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>No rebuilt support lineup for the September 25 through November 7 dates was public.</p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>No earlier change to the Loop Tour support lineup appears in the official tour schedule, artist statements or coverage through September 20, leaving no prior case on this tour for comparison.</p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative">The ticket terms described by the Massachusetts attorney general's office allow opening acts to change.<Cite s="boston-25-gillette-refund-complaints-2026-09-18" passage="opening acts are subject to change" /> Massachusetts law separately requires licensed ticket sellers to guarantee a refund when a ticket “fails to conform to its description as advertised,” unless the purchaser approved a substitution of tickets.<Cite s="massachusetts-ticket-refund-law-185d-2026-09-20" passage="the ticket fails to conform to its description as advertised unless the purchaser has pre-approved a substitution of tickets" /></p>

	        <p data-layer="narrative" data-absence>The attorney general's September 18 statement did not say whether removing an advertised opening act met the law's ticket-description test.</p>
